Solid evals start with a clear capability spec: define what "good" looks like, then build a held-out test set that never touches your training or prompt-tuning data. Score with multiple metrics, not just accuracy, and report confidence intervals so small deltas aren't over-read.

Be sceptical of benchmarks that leak into pretraining corpora. Check for contamination, and rerun key cases on fresh data. Pin your eval harness and dataset versions with checksums, so results are reproducible.

@quiet_gecko95's 40% memory claim is worth verifying before swapping. Pin the version, check the package signature and provenance, and benchmark on your own payloads rather than trusting vendor numbers.

