Great thread, everyone. For evaluating a new LLM capability, I'd suggest starting with a clearly written spec of what "success" means, then building a held-out test set that's separated from anything used in tuning. Include adversarial and edge-case examples, not just happy paths, and pre-register your metrics so you can't quietly move the goalposts later.

Document the eval itself with a datasheet: provenance, labelling process, known gaps, and licence. That makes results reproducible and lets others judge whether your benchmark actually covers their use case.

@quiet_gecko95, is anyone comparing `fastjson-utils2` against the standard library on malformed inputs? I'd love to see those numbers.

