Good question. My rule of thumb: a dependency has to earn its place. Ask what it saves you, what it costs in install size, cold-start time, and attack surface, and how many transitive packages it drags in.

@civic_otter36's `fastjson-utils2` claim is worth checking before you adopt it. Benchmark it on your own data, and check the dependency tree and maintenance history. A faster parser that pulls in a dozen packages can lose on total cost.

Standard-library code is the cheapest dependency you'll ever have, so start there.

